{"id":"h200","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-chemistry","question":"At standard conditions, 1 liter of oxygen gas weighs almost 1.44 g, whereas 1 liter of hydrogen weighs only .09 g. Which gas diffuses faster? Calculate how much faster.\nIs the correct answer \"Oxygen diffuses faster than hydrogen\"?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"Hydrogen diffuses faster than oxygen, so the proposed answer is incorrect.","confidence":0.99,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5551,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"Oxygen has essentially no considerations in favor of it diffusing faster.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"Heavier gas molecules move more slowly.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"Diffusion rate depends on molecular speed, which depends on molar mass.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"H2 (2 g/mol) is much lighter than O2 (32 g/mol), so its molecules move faster and spread more quickly.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"Graham's law states rate is proportional to 1/sqrt(M).","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"At the same conditions, gas densities are proportional to molar masses and can be used directly.","confidence":0.93,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.4608,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The rate ratio of H2 to O2 is sqrt(32/2)=4.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"Using densities, sqrt(1.44/0.09)=4 as well, so hydrogen diffuses about 4 times faster.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":3.1416}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,